{"caseId":"OD2-HO-NOCONC-2","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":72,"evidenceIds":["X5","X8","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"unknown","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E4","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["X8","E5","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"unknown","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E4","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"association-only","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["X5","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["X5"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E6"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"unknown","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E4","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-R5","answer":"unknown","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E4"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"premature","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["X5","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"preferred","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X8","E5","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":96,"evidenceIds":["E5","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"premature","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E4","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"acceptable","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E5","X8"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":78,"evidenceIds":["X8","E5","E6","X5"]}],"brief":"The rise from 0.31% to 0.41% is most likely ordinary week-to-week variation. Over the previous 26 weeks the rate averaged 0.354% and the upper edge of the normal band is 0.482%. Last week's 0.411% is inside that band. Three earlier weeks were higher (0.44, 0.47 and 0.51%), and the ones investigated turned up no fault. The burn-rate page did not fire (0.82x against a 2x threshold). Bookings were flat (41.0% and 41.2%) and support logged no complaints. Raw logs match the dashboard, so this is not a counting artifact.

The new-pod versus old-pod gap seen during the rollout is a cache warm-up effect. The same gap appeared in all five recent rollouts, including a config-only redeploy of an unchanged image. It adds only 10–20 errors per rollout, so it doesn't show v4.18 is faulty.

Next steps: don't roll back, don't move the replica back, and don't mute alerting. Muting would remove the protection that would catch a real problem. Record the week as within the normal band and apply the standard check to this week. I'm fairly confident, though I did not rule out a small effect from the replica move or partner-sync traffic."}
